(*
Issue #123: a long FLAT chain of same-precedence binary operators (no
parentheses at all) overflowed the real C++ stack in Sema's
checkExpr/checkBinary recursion instead of failing with a diagnostic.
Parser::ExprDepth (ParseExpr.cpp) only bounds NESTED parenthesized/'not'
recursion through parseFactor -- see
deeply-nested-parentheses-do-not-crash-the-compiler.pas next to this file --
and never sees a flat chain like `1+1+1+...+1` at all, since precedence
climbing parses that ITERATIVELY, not recursively; the parser hands Sema back
a left-leaning BinaryExpr tree as deep as the chain is long, and Sema is the
first thing that walks it recursively. 40000 terms is well past
Sema::MaxExprDepth (1000, SemaExpr.cpp) while comfortably past the
~5,000-30,000-term range this crashed at empirically before this fix, on
various stack sizes/builds.  Checked in verbatim rather than generated at
build time, matching deeply-nested-parentheses-do-not-crash-the-compiler.pas's
own precedent -- lit has no loop-emitting primitive, and the source is
deterministic; nobody should have to look at 40000 terms to read this file, so
the lit directives come first.

A crash surfaces as a real signal (SIGSEGV etc.), not just a nonzero exit
code -- plain `not` (without --crash) already distinguishes the two: it
fails the whole step if the wrapped command crashes via signal, rather than
treating a crash as just another kind of "the inverted command succeeded".
So the step below only passes if the compiler exits via a controlled,
non-crashing diagnostic -- proving this is a real fix, not just a report
that happens to also be a nonzero exit.
*)

(*
RUN: not %plang %s -o %t 2> %t.err
RUN: FileCheck %s < %t.err
*)

(*
CHECK: nested too deeply
*)

program p;
var x: integer;
begin x := ; writeln(x) end.
